                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 905,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 901,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Takes the inverse Fourier transform: turns a spectrum back into samples.\n\nWhatever t.fft took apart, t.ifft puts back together. The reason to go there and back is to do something in between: silence a band of frequencies, boost one, shift the phases, or multiply two spectra together (which is convolution, done the fast way).\n\nThe output is COMPLEX even when the original signal was real. For a real signal that came through t.fft untouched the imaginary part is only rounding error (around 0.0000001); t.real keeps the real part, as in the demo. If you edited the spectrum and the imaginary part is NOT tiny, the edit broke the mirror symmetry a real signal needs - edit the matching negative-frequency bin too, or work with t.rfft / t.irfft, which keep the symmetry for you.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.ifft <dim: int>\nWith no argument it transforms along the last dimension.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.ifft\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA spectrum, as t.fft makes it: complex, one value per sample. Each tensor that arrives is transformed and sent at once. A real tensor is accepted too and treated as a spectrum with no imaginary part.\n\ndim (an int box, only when you give an argument):\nt.ifft 0 transforms along dimension 0 instead of the last one. With no argument the node has no dim box and always uses the last dimension, which is what you want for one signal, or for a stack of signals one per row.\n\nnorm (menu):\nWhere the 1/n scaling goes. 'backward' (the default) leaves the forward transform unscaled and divides on the way back, so a sine of size 1 over 256 samples makes a peak of 128. 'forward' divides on the way forward (that peak becomes 0.5 - half the sine's size, the other half sits in the mirror bin). 'ortho' divides both ways by the square root of n. A forward and an inverse node must use the same setting or the round trip comes back the wrong size.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nfft tensor out:\nA complex tensor the same shape as the input: the samples.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe top plot is the signal going in; the bottom one is what comes back after t.fft and t.ifft. Drag the slider: the two always match, because the transform loses nothing.\n\nRELATED:\nt.fft is the forward transform. t.irfft is the inverse for the half spectrum t.rfft makes, and returns real samples directly. t.real and t.imag take a complex result apart.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 885,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Takes the inverse Fourier transform: turns a spectrum back into samples.\n\nWhatever t.fft took apart, t.ifft puts back together. The reason to go there and back is to do something in between: silence a band of frequencies, boost one, shift the phases, or multiply two spectra together (which is convolution, done the fast way).\n\nThe output is COMPLEX even when the original signal was real. For a real signal that came through t.fft untouched the imaginary part is only rounding error (around 0.0000001); t.real keeps the real part, as in the demo. If you edited the spectrum and the imaginary part is NOT tiny, the edit broke the mirror symmetry a real signal needs - edit the matching negative-frequency bin too, or work with t.rfft / t.irfft, which keep the symmetry for you.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.ifft <dim: int>\nWith no argument it transforms along the last dimension.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.ifft\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA spectrum, as t.fft makes it: complex, one value per sample. Each tensor that arrives is transformed and sent at once. A real tensor is accepted too and treated as a spectrum with no imaginary part.\n\ndim (an int box, only when you give an argument):\nt.ifft 0 transforms along dimension 0 instead of the last one. With no argument the node has no dim box and always uses the last dimension, which is what you want for one signal, or for a stack of signals one per row.\n\nnorm (menu):\nWhere the 1/n scaling goes. 'backward' (the default) leaves the forward transform unscaled and divides on the way back, so a sine of size 1 over 256 samples makes a peak of 128. 'forward' divides on the way forward (that peak becomes 0.5 - half the sine's size, the other half sits in the mirror bin). 'ortho' divides both ways by the square root of n. A forward and an inverse node must use the same setting or the round trip comes back the wrong size.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nfft tensor out:\nA complex tensor the same shape as the input: the samples.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe top plot is the signal going in; the bottom one is what comes back after t.fft and t.ifft. Drag the slider: the two always match, because the transform loses nothing.\n\nRELATED:\nt.fft is the forward transform. t.irfft is the inverse for the half spectrum t.rfft makes, and returns real samples directly. t.real and t.imag take a complex result apart."
                },
